  "worked_solution": "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \u27e81-3{t}^{2},4t-1\u27e9\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\u27e8\\int (1-3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (4t-1)\\,\\text{d}t\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8t-{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},2{t}^{2}-t+{C}_{2}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=\u27e8-1,1\u27e9.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\u27e82-{2}^{3}+{C}_{1},2({2}^{2})-2+{C}_{2}\u27e9 \\\\ \u27e8-1,1\u27e9 & =\u27e8-6+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}\u27e9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=5$ and ${C}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\u27e8t-{t}^{3}+5,2{t}^{2}-t-5\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =\u27e81-{1}^{3}+5,2({1}^{2})-1-5\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e85,-4\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
